of of rationality as  um  sort of based on calculation some kind  of formal consistency uh and that seems  to me  way too narrow and a very inadequate way  of thinking what it is i mean roughly  speaking um the way i think of  rationality it's  um  some kind of openness to getting new  evidence and  um and  disposition to to act and to form  beliefs in ways that are appropriate  given uh your evidence and  and that's that's something  that that i would uh see as coming in  degrees and so that you know adults are  typically um  more rational than  two-year-old children  um the two-year-old children are not  totally uh lacking in  uh in rationality um and  um  i would even think of  rationality as something that  can be found in you know in somewhat  primitive forms in in many  non-human uh animals i mean so that for  example you know when you you think of a  predator um hunting a prey i mean they  they're both  typically you know they've got to be  able to to act in some way in the light  of their evidence i mean their evidence  about where the other animal  is and about you know about where  what there is in their environment stuff  to hide behind and all those kind of  things and you know so if they're if  they were acting in  uh completely irrational ways which paid  no attention to their evidence uh that  they're not likely to withstand the the  test of evolution uh for for very long  so so this is it's actually an extremely  widespread phenomenon over a whole range  of species of course with human  rationality we've got we've got language  and we can use language to  uh you know reflect in ex explicit sort  of ways about our evidence and and and  that enables us to be to be rational in  in much more  uh sophisticated  uh ways to to recognize you know the  ways in in which our sort of initial  instincts may may actually be mistaken  and then we can correct them  but and so that's as it were an extra  layer of rationality that that we have  that isn't available in the same way to  creatures that don't have language but  basically we're just talking about  different levels of something that is uh  extremely widespread amongst living  creatures  great thank you nina  yeah i guess first of all i'd say that  everybody thinks  and i'd be very wary of anyone who would  say  otherwise  and in that sense i think we have to  understand that everybody has reasons  for the claims that they make  and i think it's often very disingenuous  when we seek to  present our opponents if you like as  being motivated by extra rational  concerns alone so and this often happens  in particularly emotive debates where we  seek to assert that the other person is  motivated by something malicious they're  motivated by hate perhaps  whereas if you actually want to have a  dialogue with somebody you have to  assume uh something like the principle  of charity which is that basically the  person  argument has they have good reasons for  why they're claiming what they claim and  you argue against that and i think we  need to have more of an understanding of  the uh sort of necessity and reality of  dialogue and the fact that people give  different reasons for different  positions and that everybody  thinks is basically a fundamental  axiom at the same time i think it's very  difficult to think for oneself even  though that seems very obvious that this  is something we all  grant ourselves  it's actually very difficult to  find out sometimes what it is that we  actually think about a particular issue  we're surrounded by opinion and doxa and  kind of a whole confusing  array of information and it often takes  time to work through  an idea or an argument and i think  reason is one of those things that helps  us to think it's not everything  i do think though that reason and  emotion for example shouldn't be opposed  if you read someone like spinoza he says  that basically emotion and this these  ideas of these kind of feelings that  they're also amenable to comprehension  so that we can actually um get a bigger  picture of why we feel the way we do why  we feel for example envy towards someone  why we feel confusion in another context  we can actually  access reason to talk about emotions so  i wouldn't necessarily oppose reason and  emotion  either  i would say that a narrow image of  rationality for example the idea that  human beings are only fundamentally  calculating uh for example like economic  rationality  is not  the sum total of how we think about  things and so i would also defend the  idea of reason as having a speculative  element which is to say asking questions  that we can't immediately answer maybe  asking questions that are very grandiose  asking questions about the cosmos about  the nature of our soul about whether god  exists or not whether our soul is  immortal you know kant talks about  reason as basically pushing us to ask  questions that we can't answer so i  think reason also has this kind of  cosmic speculative  philosophical uh dimension and it's not  always going to be able to solve those  questions but it kind of pushes us  to ask them  and in that sense i think it's very it's  something very beautiful and something  that we can engage in together in  dialogue and this is in a way what  philosophical dialogue  is all about is reasoning together it's  not something that we can necessarily  only do on our own even at the same time  as we want to try to understand what it  is that we actually think about any  particular issue so i think there should  be more reason i think that everyone has  reason i think that everyone can give  reasons and we should be able to talk  about everything that matters to us  without being shut down  so you're using reason as a synonym for  rationality here um yeah i mean if there  are  we could talk about complicated  definitions but yes let's say for the  sake of this conversation okay great  thank you sophie  hi um so i think that sometimes by  rationality people